<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jainism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jain religion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jainism is an Indian religion that teaches liberation of the soul through non-violence, truthfulness, non-attachment and strict self-discipline. Its followers revere 24 tirthankaras, the last of whom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, lived in the 6th or 5th century BCE. Divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> and Shvetambara traditions, Jains have long been prominent as merchants and patrons of temples, such as those of Palitana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jainism.jpg" alt="Jainism" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthew Logelin · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shravanbelgola_Gomateshvara_feet_prayer1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>British India was a predominantly rural society of peasant cultivators, organised by caste, religion and region, with Hindus forming the majority and Muslims the largest minority, alongside Sikhs, Christians, <b>Jains</b>, Parsis and tribal peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings presented themselves as patrons of Vedic sacrifice and of the gods Vishnu and Shiva, yet they also supported <b>Jainism</b>: the Meguti temple at Aihole, which carries the inscription of 634, is a Jain shrine, and one of the four cave temples at Badami is Jain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The early Hoysalas patronised <b>Jainism</b>, and Vishnuvardhana’s queen Shantala Devi and his general Gangaraja endowed Jain shrines at Shravanabelagola.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, attained enlightenment at Bodh Gaya in Magadha and preached at Rajagriha, where Bimbisara gave his order the Veluvana bamboo grove; Vardhamana Mahavira, the great teacher of <b>Jainism</b>, also taught in the region, and the Ajivikas of Makkhali Gosala were active there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was predominantly Hindu, with the Wodeyars patrons of Srivaishnava and Shaiva temples, including the Ranganatha temple at Srirangapatna and the Chamundeshwari temple on the hill above Mysore, while <b>Jains</b> and a growing Muslim population formed important communities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Kanchipuram was a great city of temples, monasteries and colleges; the Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang, who visited around 640, described its many Buddhist monasteries, its Hindu temples and its numerous <b>Jains</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
